{"name":"Antarctic Blue","hex":"#4b789b","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"antarctic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative spelling of Antarctic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/antarctic"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Antarctic","blocks":[{"text":"The Antarctic is the polar region of Earth that surrounds the South Pole, lying within the Antarctic Circle. It is diametrically opposite of the Arctic region around the North Pole."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Antarctic-Overview-Map-EN.tif/lossless-page1-330px-Antarctic-Overview-Map-EN.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antarctic","wikidata":"Q1555938"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/AntarcticPlate.png/500px-AntarcticPlate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/AntarcticPlate.png/1280px-AntarcticPlate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/AntarcticPlate.png/500px-AntarcticPlate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Antarctic Plate","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntarcticPlate.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Antarctica_6400px_from_Blue_Marble.jpg/500px-Antarctica_6400px_from_Blue_Marble.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Antarctica_6400px_from_Blue_Marble.jpg/1280px-Antarctica_6400px_from_Blue_Marble.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Antarctica_6400px_from_Blue_Marble.jpg/500px-Antarctica_6400px_from_Blue_Marble.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An October 2006 NASA satellite image of the Antarctic without its periphery of unattached sea ice","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntarctica_6400px_from_Blue_Marble.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Orthographic_projection_centered_on_the_Prince_Edward_Island_%28Vela_Incident%29.png/330px-Orthographic_projection_centered_on_the_Prince_Edward_Island_%28Vela_Incident%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikim